verdict:
The concept shape holds up. I compiled real generic user code against a mock built on gurobi_milp with the plan's concepts, at -fsyntax-only: a DFJ branch-and-cut with lazy and user cuts sharing one separator, a Benders cut, a cover separator with shared state, requires-clauses and the `has_user_cuts<Model> auto &` shorthand. All of it compiles, the negatives hold, and two expected-failure variants fail as they should. Keeping `has_node_relaxation_callback<Model>` for the reads and `has_user_cuts<Handle, Model>` on the handle is right. So is `void` from add_user_cut, and so is rejecting both a single callback object and a scope tag. Five things around the API are wrong or missing, and each has a concrete failure:
- **Deletion filter.** It runs a separator in every trial. A cut valid for the model, which on an infeasible model is any cut, made `compute_iis_by_deletion` return `irreducible` for a feasible set, on every seed I ran. The path was identical each time.
- **Mutex scope.** Decision 4 does not say the mutex covers every callback of the model. The plan's own data shows candidate and relaxation calls overlapping on CPLEX. One mutex shared by three CPLEX contexts did not deadlock in my runs.
- **Xpress floor.** Decision 3's 45.01.04 floor sits inside Xpress's validated range, so under the two-tier ruling the shared suite would fail on 45.01.00 to 45.01.03.
- **D4.** It is not Gurobi-only: CPLEX 22.1.2 reproduces it. For user cuts alone a late setter is harmless, so the fix belongs in solve() and should be keyed on the candidate callback.
- **Point lifetime.** On Gurobi the mapping from the candidate handle's get_solution() dies with the call (ASan); CPLEX has the same capture by reading. A separator that keeps its last point reads a dead stack frame. The new handle must not copy that pattern.

The contract wording needs these exact fixes:
- define "lazy constraints included" by what solve() may return;
- name the false infeasible;
- tell users to offer a cut again whenever it is violated rather than remember which cuts they added;
- make non-finite input droppable by the way the drop test is written;
- state that a refused cut is dropped, never thrown.

Of the maintainer decisions: 1 and 2 are correctly posed and their recommendations are right. 3 and 4 are posed wrong (Xpress floor, mutex scope). 5 and 6 each bundle two questions. 7 needs CPLEX added to D4. The plan poses no decision on the deletion filter, or on where Gurobi's PreCrush is set and restored.

findings.replacement:
// c must hold at every point solve() may return as feasible: integer, within the
// model's rows and bounds, and accepted by the candidate-solution callback (no
// lazy constraint added, no rejection). The solver may apply it anywhere or
// nowhere, and may drop it later: a later point, at this node or another, can
// violate it, so offer a cut whenever it is violated rather than remembering
// which cuts were added. A cut that removes such a point is undefined behavior,
// even one that keeps an optimum: it can give a wrong optimum at status::optimal,
// or status::infeasible on a feasible model.

auto separate = [&](auto & handle) {
    auto x = handle.get_solution();
    for(const auto & S : violated_subtours(x)) {
        auto cut = xsum(leaving(S), arc_var) >= 1;
        if constexpr(has_user_cuts<std::remove_cvref_t<decltype(handle)>, Model>)
            handle.add_user_cut(cut);
        else
            handle.add_lazy_constraint(cut);
    }
};
model.set_candidate_solution_callback(separate);  // feasibility
model.set_node_relaxation_callback(separate);     // strength
model.solve();
(This compiles in final_api_mock.cpp, solve_atsp.)
